  summary: [
    "DNA is the genetic material of all living organisms, carrying a universal genetic code; some viruses use RNA instead, but viruses are not considered living, so the statement still holds.",
    "A nucleotide is built from a nitrogenous base, a pentose sugar (deoxyribose in DNA, ribose in RNA) and a phosphate group, joined by condensation reactions; bases divide into purines (adenine, guanine) and pyrimidines (cytosine, thymine, or uracil in RNA).",
    "Nucleotides condense into long polynucleotides with a uniform sugar&ndash;phosphate backbone (linked by 3'&ndash;5' phosphodiester bonds) and projecting bases whose varying sequence, read as a triplet code of codons, specifies the 20 amino acids used to build proteins.",
    "RNA is a shorter, usually single-stranded polymer using ribose and uracil in place of DNA's deoxyribose and thymine, occurring as mRNA, tRNA and rRNA, each with a distinct role in protein synthesis.",
    "DNA forms a double helix of two antiparallel strands, held together by hydrogen bonds between complementary base pairs (A&ndash;T with 2 H-bonds, C&ndash;G with 3 H-bonds) &mdash; a structure first modelled by Watson and Crick in 1953 using evidence including Rosalind Franklin's X-ray diffraction images.",
    "Complementary base pairing lets the sequence of one DNA strand determine the sequence of the other, enabling both accurate replication and expression of genetic information; genes occupy fixed loci on chromosomes and may exist as different alleles, inherited on homologous chromosomes from each parent.",
    "DNA's four-letter code gives it a virtually limitless capacity to store information: gene number (e.g. ~20 000 in humans, ~41 500 in rice) and genome size (from 3.5 kb in a virus to 150 Gb in a plant) both vary hugely between species, independently of organismal complexity.",
    "The genetic code's 64 codons mean the same thing in nearly all organisms, supporting universal common ancestry; conserved sequences persist largely unchanged across generations, and synonymous mutations can alter a DNA sequence without altering its protein product.",
    "<strong>HL:</strong> the antiparallel, directional structure of DNA (5' to 3' vs 3' to 5') governs how replication, transcription and translation proceed, while strict purine-to-pyrimidine pairing gives every base pair the same width, making the helix stable and uniform regardless of sequence.",
    "<strong>HL:</strong> because DNA is so much longer than the nucleus that contains it, it is packaged by winding around histone proteins to form nucleosomes, which coil further into chromatin and then into the condensed metaphase chromosome; prokaryotes, with much smaller genomes, instead have 'naked', unpackaged DNA.",
    "<strong>HL:</strong> the Hershey&ndash;Chase experiment used radioactive labels specific to DNA (<sup>32</sup>P) and protein (<sup>35</sup>S) to show that only a bacteriophage's DNA enters its host cell and directs the production of new viruses &mdash; key evidence that DNA, not protein, is the genetic material.",
    "<strong>HL:</strong> Chargaff's rules (A = T, G = C, and purines = pyrimidines) across diverse organisms gave Watson and Crick the numerical evidence for correct base pairing, and falsified Levene's earlier, incorrect 'tetranucleotide' hypothesis of DNA structure."
  ]
